WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.281
Are there benefits
to the osteoarthritis supplements

00:00:03.281 --> 00:00:05.113
glucosamine and chondroitin?

00:00:05.113 --> 00:00:07.011
Watch this video and the next one

00:00:07.011 --> 00:00:09.714
to see what the science says.

00:00:09.923 --> 00:00:16.951
"The Risks and Benefits of
Glucosamine for Osteoarthritis"

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.160
Global sales
of glucosamine supplements

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.080
is estimated at $2 billion.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.820
Glucosamine is a precursor
to two of the major components

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.600
of cartilage,

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.400
though at the marginal
blood levels

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.600
achieved by supplementation,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.000
one would not expect it
to contribute much

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.920
to cartilage formation directly.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.560
There are marked inconsistencies

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.240
in the clinical research
literature

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.160
as to whether it works at all.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.220
The most potent predictor
of trial results?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.320
Industry funding.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.440
Studies sponsored
by the product makers themselves

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.900
showed that their patented
products were beneficial,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.480
but independently-funded studies

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.940
showed glucosamine
had no effect.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.640
This has raised
serious concerns

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.380
about "publication bias,"

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.780
the suspicion
that the glucosamine industry

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.840
quietly shelved rather than
published any studies

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:07.740
that didn't go their way

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.980
to give an overly rosy picture
in the medical literature.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.780
This is what led in part

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.000
to the current American College
of Rheumatology guidelines

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.840
strongly recommending against
the use of glucosamine.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.960
The proscription
against glucosamine

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.720
is echoed by some expert
consensus guidelines,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.940
for example, the American
Academy of Orthopedic Surgeons

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.540
and Osteoarthritis
Research Society International,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.600
but not others,
such as the European Society

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.280
for Clinical
and Economic Aspects

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.960
of Osteoporosis,
Osteoarthritis,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.560
and Musculoskeletal Diseases,
which allows an exception

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.100
for pharmaceutical-grade
glucosamine.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.520
Only this patented so-called
"crystalline" glucosamine

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.800
sold as a prescription-only drug
in Europe

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.360
has been decisively shown

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.180
to improve osteoarthritic
joint pain or function,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.240
whereas the over-the-counter
glucosamine supplements

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.160
sold in the U.S.
offered mixed results

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.840
with no overall effect,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.200
presumably because of
quality control analyses

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.060
by Consumer Reports and others

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.460
found glucosamine supplements
sometimes contained

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.740
no glucosamine at all.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.220
An investigation of over
a dozen glucosamine supplements

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.300
found that only one contained
the claimed dose on the label.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.320
So, maybe the inconsistency
in findings

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.720
is less a matter
of funding bias

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.880
and more a reflection
of the efficacy

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.980
of using genuine glucosamine.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.980
I'd feel more confident
in the industry results

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.660
if they were more transparent

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.100
with their participant-level
results.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.720
A request for data sharing was
reportedly denied by the owner

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.940
of the crystalline
glucosamine trials,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.740
which "raises concerns

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.200
about the robustness
of their study findings."

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.880
Regardless, the effect size
of the pain relief

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.880
found even in the best studies
of crystalline glucosamine

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.980
can be considered
relatively small.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.500
Effect size can be quantified

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.840
as a "standardized
mean difference."

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.020
An effect size of 0.2
is considered small,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.380
0.5 moderate, and 0.8 large.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.540
The effect size
of the best studies

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.360
on crystalline glucosamine,
0.27,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.640
representing a small effect.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.660
So, prescription glucosamine
works better than Tylenol,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.820
but on par
with placebo injections

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.880
and less than NSAID drugs
like ibuprofen.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.760
Now, glucosamine is safer
than NSAIDs,

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.860
but the tantalizing possibility

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.580
is that it could offer more
than just symptomatic relief.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.900
Two industry-funded
randomized controlled trials

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.180
found that those
with knee osteoarthritis

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.580
randomized to take
the prescription glucosamine

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:42.120
for three years

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.240
experienced less progression
of their disease

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.200
compared to placebo,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.240
as quantified
by X-ray measurements.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.620
Those on the glucosamine
had a 62 percent reduction

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.360
in osteoarthritis progression

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.460
as measured
by joint space narrowing

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.740
compared to those randomized
instead to placebo.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.080
However, two subsequent
non-industry-funded studies

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.460
found no benefit

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.560
on osteoarthritis disease
progression,

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.600
resulting in no benefit overall

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.580
when all four studies
were combined.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:15.300
But how's this for a purported
potential side effect:

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.280
a longer life?

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.180
Glucosamine supplementation
extends the lifespan

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.000
of both microscopic worms
and aging mice.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.920
What about people?

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.340
In 2012, the Vitamins
and Lifestyle cohort

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.440
was the first to report
on the relationship

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.480
between glucosamine use
and mortality.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.080
About 75,000
Washington State residents

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.540
aged 50 through 76
were followed

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.680
for about seven years,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.520
and those taking glucosamine
were 18 percent less likely

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.320
to die in that period.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.540
In 2020,
two other cohort studies

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:49.320
were published on the matter,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.440
including the enormous
UK Biobank study

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.060
that followed nearly
a half million people

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:54.900
for nine years.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.040
Glucosamine users were
15 percent less likely to die,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.740
and in a 2020
national U.S. study,

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.740
users had 27 percent lower risk
of death.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.140
Since glucosamine use
has been associated

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.080
with lower CRP blood levels,
researchers suggested

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.360
this may be
an anti-inflammatory effect,

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.000
but when actually put
to the test,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.820
glucosamine doesn't appear
to affect CRP at all.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.740
A more likely explanation

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.800
may be the so-called
"healthy user effect."

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.280
Those who choose
to take supplements

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.100
tend to be healthier
than those who don't.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.920
Studies show
dietary supplement users

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.500
are more likely to be female,
more educated,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.080
and make healthier
diet and lifestyle choices,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.240
such as exercising more
and smoking less,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.440
and being less sick.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.280
So, presumably, that's why
observational studies found

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.220
that those who take vitamins
may have lower mortality rates,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.280
but when put to the test
in interventional studies,

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.960
there was no benefit or worse.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.240
One of the most dramatic
examples

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.800
of the healthy user effect

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.920
is the finding that elders
who take flu vaccines

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.980
appear to cut their risk
of all-cause winter mortality

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.240
by 50 percent,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.140
when seasonal flu itself
rarely causes

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.840
more than 10 percent
of excess winter deaths.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.240
That's why randomized
controlled trials

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.420
are so critical to establishing
cause and effect,

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:19.500
and we have no such data
as of yet for glucosamine.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.260
The cohort studies
did try to adjust

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.140
for factors such as age,
obesity, exercise education,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.400
and smoking status,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.020
but it's not possible to control

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.540
for unmeasured
lifestyle confounders.

